i mean in the beginning i just  tried to talk myself out of starting  this business because  it really didn't make sense in in that  time  of the world but the more i looked into  climate change the more i understood  what a humongous problem it is gonna be  and i just i just had no choice  about two years ago nobody was really  interested they didn't think that it  would be  a good business they didn't really see  the money in it  that's all changed so rapidly everybody  and their grandma is calling themselves  a climate investor now which is  fantastic  i'm henrietta moon i'm the ceo and  co-founder of carboculture  and carboculture is removing atmospheric  carbon  by turning biomass waste into highly  stable carbon that remains stable for  1000 years  most life on earth is carbon and it's  our fourth most abundant element  so so it's not bad per se we just have  it in the wrong place  in too much scale so we need to think of  the most elegant way to actually  bring down that carbon from the  atmosphere and put it to use somewhere  where we can  use it better this challenge is at the  core  of carboculture's mission the helsinki  based startup is focused on solving the  earth's carbon problem  while offering a viable business model  one that didn't even exist  at the beginning of her career i ended  up in this crazy program that was  three month intense program at nasa ames  in california and that's where i met  my co-founder chris karstens back then  there was no price on carbon  emission trading was at maybe five euros  a ton and  nobody was really interested in pursuing  that as a business model  we got started properly in 2017. today  the carbon market is trading at  over 10 times that so times have changed  henrietta and her team turned to nature  itself for inspiration  on how to make this model both effective  and profitable  every year nature draws down carbon in  the form of  photosynthesis into all our biomass  trees and plantations and crops and  everything you see  but naturally when that biomass like the  trees die  they decompose and re-enter the  atmosphere again and that's the natural  carbon cycle  at carboculture we were trying to figure  out what is the most elegant way to  actually draw down some of this carbon  and instead of trying to invent machines  to take out parts per million from the  atmosphere  we decided that millions of years of  nature already knows how to do it  so if you see a forest today and in 500  years  it'll essentially be the same size it's  not going to 10x  its carbon removal capacity or or grow  10 times  larger so we have these solar powered  allies  who use photosynthesis and turn this  carbon from the atmosphere into biomass  and we take that biomass such as  nutshells or peach pits or  or tree waste and we put it into our  patented reactor and we  rapidly take it to ultra high heat where  it carbonizes  it doesn't decompose it's not eaten by  microbes or anything  and and we can store it safely in the  soil by doing this the  carbon that's almost pure carbon remains  stable from 1 000 years to let's say  maybe up to a million years  [Music]  their first facility resides in  california which operates  as a proof of concept to demonstrate the  process  basically we put biomass in there and  and we put them inside we take them to  ultra high temperature about a thousand  degrees celsius for a split  second and in that time the biomass  carbonizes  so it turns into a charcoal like form  you can  mix it into compost or put it in the  soil or use it for water filtration or  just  bury it in the ground in an old mine  shaft or something  [Music]  our technology is like on a basic level  the same thing  as turning wood into a carbon is is  something that everybody can do in their  home fireplace right  but now if you want to make it like  ultra ultra ultra efficient  and actually store the carbon away now  it gets a little bit more difficult  but the kind of principle is the same  right now our batch takes about two  hours  for half a ton so we can do about 500  pounds per hour  let's say of biomass now that they've  mastered the concept  it's really just a matter of scaling it  up by a factor of  a million or so if we put a million of  these systems around the world we can  actually use part of the energy in the  biomass itself  to power these systems and in this way  we're helping the green transition  of the grid instead of hindering it this  next scale up at scale would remove  about  13 000 tons of carbon dioxide annually  so so make a few thousand tons of  biocarbon and  that's a big step for us though  carboculture has recently raised 6.2  million dollars in seed financing  it will take a lot more funding to get  carboculture's vision to scale  this herculean effort will require  investments from both public  and private sectors and now that carbon  capture programs like this  are beginning to get traction henrietta  welcomes the competition  if we look at the big picture we have a  north star  mission of a gigaton of removal which is  one billion tons  and it's a real insanity target it's  definitely not easy  it's light years away from where we are  today but for us it means that we also  need to get to that kind of  scale and there's probably going to be  10 to 100 companies like  us in maybe a decade from now that will  be  trying to get to that gargantuan scale  that is needed
 

